ce the 3.5 release of the CUDA Core Compute Libraries (CCCL). Highlights include public tuning APIs for CUB, reproducible floating-point scans, batched Top-K selection, and reductions that accept problem sizes stored on the GPU.\nL153: ## Public CUB Tuning APIs\nL154: \nL155: CCCL 3.5 exposes public tuning policies for CUB device-wide algorithms. Applications can customize parameters such as threads per block, items per thread, vectorization, and algorithm selection by passing a policy selector through `cuda::execution::tune(...)` in an execution environment.\nL156: Policy selectors choose tuning parameters for a GPU compute capability and return an algorithm-specific policy such as `cub::ReducePolicy` or `cub::ScanPolicy`. This provides a supported way to specialize CUB for a workload through the public device-wide APIs.\nL157: For example, this example policy below uses 256 threads per block and selects the number of items per thread based on compute capability:\nL158: \nL159:     struct ReduceTuning {\nL160:       __host__ __device__ constexpr cub::ReducePolicy\nL161:       operator()(cuda::compute_capability cc) const {\nL162:         auto pass = cub::ReducePassPolicy{\nL163:           .threads_per_block = 256,\nL164:           .items_per_thread = cc >= cuda::compute_capability{10, 0} ? 20 : 16,\nL165:           .vec_size = 4,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCUDA 11 Features Revealed | NVIDIA Technical Blog (https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/cuda-11-features-revealed/)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view0\",\"pattern\":\"reduction\"}); Total lines: 375\nL194: #### Thread collectives\nL195: \nL196: Here are some of the enhancements that CUDA 11 adds to 【24†cooperative groups】, introduced in CUDA 9. Cooperative Groups is a collective programming mode that aims to enable you to explicitly express granularities at which the threads can communicate. This enables new patterns of cooperative parallelism within CUDA.\nL197: In CUDA 11, cooperative group collectives expose new A100 hardware features and add several API enhancements. For more information about the complete list of changes, see the 【25†CUDA Programming Guide†docs.nvidia.com】.\nL198: A100 introduces a new reduce instruction that operates on the data provided by each thread. This is exposed as a new collective using cooperative groups, which provides a portable abstraction that can be used on older architectures as well. The reduce operation supports arithmetic (for example, add), and logical (for example, AND) operations. The following code example shows the reduce collective.\nL199: \nL200:     `// Simple Reduction Sum\nL201:     #include <cooperative_groups/reduce.h>\nL202: \nL203:        ...\nL204:        const int threadId = cta.thread_rank();\nL205:        int val = A[threadId];\nL206:        // reduce across tiled partition\nL207:        reduceArr[threadId] = cg::reduce(tile, val, cg::plus<int>());\nL208:        // synchronize partition\nL209:        cg::sync(cta);\nL210:        // accumulate sum using a leader and return sum`\nL211: Cooperative groups provide collective operations (`labeled_partition`) that partition the parent group into one-dimensional subgroups within which the threads are coalesced. This is particularly helpful for control flow that attempts to keep track of active threads through basic blocks of conditional statements.\nL212: For example, multiple partitions can be formed out of a warp-level group (that is not constrained to powers of 2) using `labeled_partition` and used in an atomic add operation. The `labeled_partition` API operation evaluates a condition label and assigns threads that have the same value for the label into the same group.\nL213: The following code example shows custom thread partitions:\nL214: \nL215:     `// Get current active threads (that is, coalesced_threads())\nL216:     cg::coalesced_group active = cg::coalesced_threads();\nL217: \nL218:     // Match threads with the same label using match_any()\nL219:     int bucket = active.match_any(value);\nL220: \nL221:     cg::coalesced_group subgroup = cg::labeled_partition(active, bucket);\nL222: \nL223:     // Choose a leader for each partition (for example, thread_rank = 0)\nL224:     //\nL225:     if (subgroup.thread_rank() == 0) {\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNVIDIA Ampere GPU Architecture Tuning Guide :: CUDA Toolkit Documentation (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/11.0_GA/ampere-tuning-guide/index.html)\nciteturn10view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view0\",\"pattern\":\"Last updated\"}); Total lines: 167\nL19:         * 【8†1.4.1.1. Occupancy】\nL20: \nL21:         * 【9†1.4.1.2. Asynchronous Data Copy from Global Memory to Shared Memory】\nL22: \nL23:         * 【10†1.4.1.3. Hardware Acceleration for Split Arrive/Wait Barrier】\nL24: \nL25:         * 【11†1.4.1.4. Warp level support for Reduction Operations】\nL26: \nL27:         * 【12†1.4.1.5. Improved Tensor Core Operations】\nL28: \nL29:       * 【13†1.4.2. Memory System】\nL30: \nL31:         * 【14†1.4.2.1. Increased Memory Capacity and High Bandwidth Memory】\nL32: \nL33:         * 【15†1.4.2.2. Increased L2 capacity and L2 Residency Controls】\nL34:         * 【16†1.4.2.3. Unified Shared Memory/L1/Texture Cache】\nL35: \nL36:       * 【17†1.4.3. Third Generation NVLink】\nL37: \nL38:   * 【18†A. Revision History】\nL39: ## Search Results\nL40: \nL41: NVIDIA Ampere GPU Architecture Tuning Guide (【19†PDF】) - v11.0.194 (【20†older†developer.nvidia.com】) - Last updated July 2, 2020 - Send Feedback (CUDAIssues@nvidia.com)\nL42: \nL43: ## 【21†Tuning CUDA Applications for NVIDIA Ampere GPU Architecture】\nL44: \nL45: The programming guide for tuning CUDA Applications for GPUs based on the NVIDIA Ampere GPU Architecture.\nL46: \nL47: ## 【2†1. NVIDIA Ampere GPU Architecture Tuning Guide】\nL48: ### 【3†1.1. NVIDIA Ampere GPU Architecture】\nL49: The NVIDIA Ampere GPU architecture is NVIDIA's latest architecture for CUDA compute applications. The NVIDIA Ampere GPU architecture retains and extends the same CUDA programming model provided by previous NVIDIA GPU architectures such as Turing and Volta, and applications that follow the best practices for those architectures should typically see speedups on the NVIDIA A100 GPU without any code changes.\nL50: This guide summarizes the ways that an application can be fine-tuned to gain additional speedups by leveraging the NVIDIA Ampere GPU architecture's features.【22†^{1}】\nL51: For further details on the programming features discussed in this guide, please refer to the 【23†CUDA C++ Programming Guide】.\nL52: ### 【4†1.2. CUDA Best Practices】\nL53: \nL54: The performance guidelines and best practices described in the 【23†CUDA C++ Programming Guide】 and the 【24†CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide】 apply to all CUDA-capable GPU architectures. Programmers must primarily focus on following those recommendations to achieve the best performance.\nL55: \nL56: The high-priority recommendations from those guides are as follows:\nL57:   * Find ways to parallelize sequential code.\nL58:   * Minimize data transfers between the host and the device.\nL59:   * Adjust kernel launch configuration to maximize device utilization.\nL60:   * Ensure global memory accesses are coalesced.\nL61:   * Minimize redundant accesses to global memory whenever possible.\nL62:   * Avoid long sequences of diverged execution by threads within the same warp.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nperf(mod_arith): Goldilocks add dominates hash kernels — can NVPTX use the carry flag? · Issue #409 · fractalyze/prime-ir · GitHub (https://github.com/fractalyze/prime-ir/issues/409)\nciteturn10view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"pattern\":\"not planned\"}); Total lines: 371\nL99:     * AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. 【61†fractalyze】\nL116:   2. 【62†prime-ir】\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues31 (31)】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests5 (5)】\nL122:   * 【65†Discussions】\nL123:   * 【66†Actions】\nL124:   * 【67†Projects】\nL125:   * 【68†Security and quality】\nL126:   * 【69†Insights】\nL127: \nL128: More items\nL129: \nL130: # perf(mod_arith): Goldilocks add dominates hash kernels — can NVPTX use the carry flag? #409\nL131: \nL132: New issue\nL133: \nL134: Copy link\nL135: \nL136: New issue\nL137: \nL138: Copy link\nL139: \nL140: Closed as not planned\nL141: \nL142: Closed as not planned\nL143: \nL144: 【70†perf(mod_arith): Goldilocks add dominates hash kernels — can NVPTX use the carry flag?】#409\nL145: \nL146: Copy link\nL147: ## Description\nL148: \nL149: 【71†erkhesbold2005-blip】\nL150: \nL151: opened 【72†on Jul 29, 2026】\nL152: \nL153: Issue body actions\nL154: \nL155: ### Pre-submission checklist\nL156: \nL157:   * [Input] I have searched the existing issues and this is not a duplicate.\nL158: ### Context / problem\nL159: \nL160: A Goldilocks addition chain reduces after every term, which makes modular\nL161: addition — not multiplication — the dominant cost in hash-heavy kernels.\nL162: \nL163: Measured on the Poseidon2 width-16 permutation (the kernel behind every merkle\nL164: tree in zisk-zorch's prover, RTX 5090, dumped PTX from the `frx` GPU plugin):\nL165: \nL166:     `831  setp        4792 instructions total\nL167:     828  selp\nL168:     148  mul.hi.u64\nL169:     `\nL170: ~3,300 of 4,792 instructions are the add-and-carry-fold pattern, against 148\nL171: multiplies. The emitted idiom per addition is:\nL172: \nL173:     `add.s64     %rd56, %rd55, %rd17        // a + b, wraps mod 2^64\nL174:     setp.lt.u64 %p7,   %rd56, %rd55        // did it wrap?\nL175:     selp.b64    %rd57, 4294967295, 0, %p7  // if so we owe +ε (ε = 2^32−1)\nL176:     add.s64     %rd58, %rd57, %rd56        // fold ε back in\nL177:     `\nL178: This is already the good path — `ConvertAdd`'s `wantLazyGl64` branch, which\nL179: avoids the canonicalizing subtract. The problem is not that it is unreduced, it\nL180: is that the fold happens per term and serially: an MDS row summing k terms\nL181: emits k carry-folds in a dependency chain (`rd56→rd58→rd59→rd61→rd64…`), so the\nL182: cost is 4 instructions and one serialized latency per term.\nL323: One more number worth recording, because it reframes the original problem: in that benchmark the current form sustains ~6.8 G adds/s = ~27 T instructions/s, roughly half of this device's integer-op peak. The Goldilocks add lowering is not inefficient — in isolation it runs well.\nL324: That means the permutation's ~5–6% overall ALU utilization is not attributable to how additions are lowered. Both hypotheses in this issue are now disproven by measurement, and the remaining explanation is the permutation's own structure — dependency chains through the rounds and register pressure limiting how many permutations stay in flight.\nL325: Closing this as measured-and-rejected. The live question moves to the Poseidon2 emitter's occupancy and ILP, which is a different component; recording the numbers here so nobody re-derives them. Reproducers: `gen_carry_bench.py` + `run_carry_bench.py`.\nL326: \nL327:   4. 【71†erkhesbold2005-blip】\nL328: \nL329: closed this as 【77†not planned】【78†on Jul 30, 2026】\nL330: \nL331: to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? 【79†Sign in to comment】\nL332: ## Metadata\nL333: \nL334: ## Metadata\nL335: \nL336: ### Assignees\nL337: \nL338: No one assigned\nL339: \nL340: ### Labels\nL341: